mat's Quotients And Related Arithmetic Functions, PNAS 1945 31 (1) pp. 55-60.\n\nH. S. Vandiver, New Types Of Congruences Involving Bernoulli Numbers and Fermat's Quotient, PNAS 1948 34 (3) pp. 103-110.\n\nH. S. Vandiver, On Congruences Which Relate The Fermat And Wilson Quotients To The Bernoulli Numbers, PNAS 1949 35 (6) pp. 332-337.\n\nFORMULA\n\nFrom Alexander Adamchuk, Oct 01 2006: (Start)\n\na(n) = 3*A096060(n) for n > 2.\n\na(n) = 3*A001045(prime(n)-1)/prime(n) for n > 1. (End)\n\na(n) = Sum_{i=0..(p-3)/2} 2^i*(p-i-2)!/((i+1)!*(p-2*(i+1))!) where p = prime(n), for n >= 2. - Vladimir Pletser, Jan 26 2023\n\nMAPLE\n\nA007663:= n-> map(p-> (2^(p-1)-1)/p, ithprime(n)):\n\nseq(A007663(n), n=2..22); # Jani Melik, Jan 24 2011\n\nMATHEMATICA\n\nA007663[n_Integer?Positive]:=(-1+2^(Prime[n]-1))/Prime[n]/; (n>1) (* Enrique Pérez Herrero, Sep 08 2010 *)\n\nTable[(2^(n-1)-1)/n, {n, Prime[Range[2, 20]]}] (* Harvey P. Dale, Nov 07 2016 *)\n\nPROG\n\n(PARI)\n\nforprime(p=3, 100, print1((2^(p-1)-1)/p \", \")) \\\\ Satish Bysany, Mar 11 2017\n\nCROSSREFS\n\nCf. A002322, A001917, A096060, A001045.\n\nKEYWORD\n\nnonn,easy,nice\n\nAUTHOR\n\nN. J. A. Sloane, Sep 19 1994\n\nSTATUS\n\napproved--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Williams_G in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AWilliams_G+in%3Amath)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; We generalize a theorem of Odoni and Cremona to show that for a fixed defining word, whose corresponding representer polynomial has an irreducible factor that is not cyclotomic and not equal to $\\pm t$, there are at most finitely many $n$ for which the corresponding $n$-generator cyclically presented group has free abelianisation. ... We conjecture that if a Gilbert-Howie group is a LOG group, then it is a Sieradski group, and prove this in certain cases (in particular, for fixed $m$, the conjecture can only be false for finitely many $n$). ... Jul 18 2017 math.CO arXiv:1707.05294v2 ... @misc{1707.05294, author = {Leah Wrenn Berman and Déborah Oliveros and Gordon I. ... The theory of cyclically presented groups informs our methods and we extend part of this theory (namely, on connections with polynomial resultants) to \"bicyclically presented groups\" that arise naturally in our analysis.\n\n  * Cyclic pseudo-Loupekine snarks\n\nLeah Wrenn Berman, Déborah Oliveros, Gordon I. Williams\n\nJul 18 2017 math.CO arXiv:1707.05294v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1707.05294, author = {Leah Wrenn Berman and Déborah Oliveros and Gordon I.~Williams}, title = {{C}yclic pseudo-{L}oupekine snarks}, year = {2017}, eprint = {1707.05294}, note = {arXiv:1707.05294v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIn 1976, Loupekine introduced (via Isaacs) a very general way of constructing new snarks from old snarks by cyclically connecting multipoles constructed from smaller snarks. In this paper, we generalize Loupekine's construction to produce a variety of snarks which can be drawn with $m$-fold rotational symmetry for $m\\geq 3$ (and often, $m$ odd), constructed as $\\mathbb{Z}_{m}$ lifts of \\emphvoltage graphs with certain properties; we call these snarks \\emphcyclic pseudo-Loupekine snarks. In particular, we discuss three infinite families of snarks which can be drawn with $\\mathbb{Z}_{m}$ rotational symmetry whose smallest element is constructed from 3 snarks with 3-fold rotational symmetry on 28 vertices; one family has the property that the oddness of the family increases with $m$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA219539 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A219539/internal)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.0 years ago; Crawled: last year; Svinin, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.05387\">On some class of sums</a>, arXiv:1610.05387 [math.CO], 2016.\n\n(Greetings from The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences!)\n\nA219539\n\nT(n,k) is the number of k-points on the left side of a crosscut of simple symmetric n-Venn diagram.\n\n`%I #27 Nov 05 2025 15:22:23 `\n\n`%S 1,1,1,1,2,3,2,1,1,4,11,19,23,19,11,4,1,1,5,17,38,61,71,61,38,17,5,1, `\n\n`%T 1,7,33,107,257,471,673,757,673,471,257,107,33,7,1,1,8,43,161,451,977, `\n\n`%U 1675,2303,2559,2303,1675,977,451,161,43,8,1 `\n\n`%N T(n,k) is the number of k-points on the left side of a crosscut of simple symmetric n-Venn diagram. `\n\n`%C A crosscut of a Venn diagram is defined as a segment of a curve which sequentially \"cuts\" (i.e., intersects) every other curve without repetition. `\n\n`%C For n=2 and 3, there are 4 and 6 crosscuts respectively. `\n\n`%C For n>3, there are either n crosscuts or none. `\n\n`%C A k-point in a simple monotone Venn diagram is defined as being an intersection point that is incident to two k-regions. `\n\n`%C The corresponding row sums are 3, 9, 93, .... (that is A007663). `\n\n`%H K. Mamakani and F. Ruskey, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.6452\">A New Rose: The First Simple Symmetric 11-Venn Diagram</a>, arXiv:1207.6452 [cs.CG], 2012. `\n\n`%H Andrei K. Svinin, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.05387\">On some class of sums</a>, arXiv:1610.05387 [math.CO], 2016. See p. 11. `\n\n`%F For 1<=k<n-1, T(n, k) = (binomial(n-1, k) + (-1)^(k+1))/n, with n>=5 being prime. `\n\n`%F T(n, k) - T(n, k-1) = (A000108(k-1) + 2*(-1)^(k+1))/n. `\n\n`%e T(n, k) is defined for n>=5 being prime: `\n\n`%e 5: 1, 1, 1, `\n\n`%e 7: 1, 2, 3, 2, 1, `\n\n`%e 11: 1, 4, 11, 19, 23, 19, 11, 4, 1, `\n\n`%e ... `\n\n`%o (PARI) a(m) = {for (n=5, m, if (isprime(n), for (k=1, n-2,if (k==1, rk =1, rk = (binomial(n-1, k)+ (-1)^(k+1))/n);print1(rk, \", \"););););} `\n\n`%Y Cf. A000108, A007663. `\n\n`%K nonn,tabf `\n\n`%O 5,5 `\n\n`%A _Michel Marcus_, Nov 22 2012 `--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Mayers_A in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AMayers_A+in%3Amath)\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Aug 06 2018 math.CO arXiv:1808.01008v1 ... @misc{1808.01008, author = {Vincent E.\n\n2 results for au:Mayers_A in:math\n\nShow all abstracts\n\n  * Statistics on integer partitions arising from seaweed algebras\n\nVincent Coll, Andrew Mayers, Nick Mayers\n\nSep 26 2018 math.CO arXiv:1809.09271v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1809.09271, author = {Vincent Coll and Andrew Mayers and Nick Mayers}, title = {{S}tatistics on integer partitions arising from seaweed algebras}, year = {2018}, eprint = {1809.09271}, note = {arXiv:1809.09271v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nUsing the index theory of seaweed algebras, we explore various new integer partition statistics. We find relations to some well-known varieties of integer partitions as well as a surprising periodicity result.\n\n  * On compositions associated to seasweed subalgebras of sl(n)\n\nVincent E. Coll, Jr, Aria Dougherty, Matthew Hyatt, Andrew W. Mayers, Nick W. Mayers\n\nAug 06 2018 math.CO arXiv:1808.01008v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1808.01008, author = {Vincent E.~Coll and Jr and Aria Dougherty and Matthew Hyatt and Andrew W.~Mayers and Nick W.~Mayers}, title = {{O}n compositions associated to seasweed subalgebras of sl(n)}, year = {2018}, eprint = {1808.01008}, note = {arXiv:1808.01008v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nA standard seaweed subalgebra of $A_{n-1}=\\mathfrak{sl}(n)$ may be parametrized by a pair of compositions of the positive integer $n$. For all $n$ and certain $k(n)$, we provide closed-form formulas and the generating functions for $C(n,k)$ -- the number of parametrizing pairs which yield a seaweed subalgebra of $\\mathfrak{sl}(n)$ of index $k$. Our analysis sets the framework for addressing similar questions in the other classical families.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) On compositions associated to seasweed subalgebras of sl(n) (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/326853258_On_compositions_associated_to_seasweed_subalgebras_of_sln)\nciteturn2search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.3 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.1808.01008 ... arXiv:1808.01008v1 [math.CO] 2 Aug 2018\n\narXiv:1808.01008v1 [math.CO] 2 Aug 2018\n\nOn compositions associated to seaweed subalgebras of sl(n)\n\nVincent E. Coll, Jr.*, Aria Dougherty*, Matthew Hyatt**, Andrew W. Mayers , and Nick W. Mayers*\n\n*Department of Mathematics, Lehigh University, Bethlehem, PA, 18015\n\n**Department of Mathematics, Pace University, NY, USA\n\nAugust 6, 2018\n\nAbstract\n\nA standard seaweed subalgebra of A n−1=sl(n) may be parametrized by a pair of compositions of\n\nthe positive integer n. For all n and certain k(n), we provide closed-form formulas and the generating\n\nfunctions for C(n, k) – the number of parametrizing pairs which yield a seaweed subalgebra of sl(n) of\n\nindex k. Our analysis sets the framework for addressing similar questions in the other classical families.\n\nKeywords Seaweed algebras, index of a Lie algebra, meanders, generating functions\n\nMathematics Sub ject Classiﬁcation (2000) 17B20, 05E15\n\nContents\n\n1 Introduction 2\n\n2 Preliminaries 3\n\n2.1 Meanders . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 3\n\n2.2 Homotopy Type ........................................... 4\n\n3 C(n, k)- Formulas and Generating Functions 4\n\n3.1 C(n, n −1) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 5\n\n3.2 C(n, n −2) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 5\n\n3.3 C(n, n −3) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 6\n\n4 More Formulas 7\n\n4.1 Linear GCD Formulas for the Index ................................ 7\n\n4.2 C 2,1(n, k). . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 8\n\n4.3 C 2,2(n, k). . . 